REWRITTEN against trunk dc4306f29. The mechanic is unchanged and still the emitter's own: codegen/render.py:1329 writes `Base._branch_models = (...)` plus `Base.model_rebuild(force=True)` at module level after the branch classes exist -- 151 lines in 135 modules of src/adcp/types/_schemas/.

BUT THE READING THIS ROW REPORTS DOES NOT EXIST HERE, which is why it is not a residue of anything today:

* pyproject's ruff config selects E, F, I, N, W, UP only -- SLF001 is not enabled;
* `extend-exclude` lists `src/adcp/types/_schemas/`, so the generated tree is outside ruff's population regardless;
* there are NO per-file-ignores in pyproject, so the bead's 'tests/* already carries a per-file ignore for SLF001' is wrong;
* the configuration it measured (0f2f9a93) is not an object in this repository.

So this row is owed only once adcp-client-python-83c wires the linter AND the configuration selects SLF001 over a population that includes the emitted tree. Whoever does that re-measures first; the figures to beat are 151/135, and the sibling `_ROOT_REQUIRED`/`_BRANCH_DISCRIMINATOR` lines are now in-body ClassVars (0 module-level assignments), so that half of the 'measure first' is already answered. The three candidates (a public installer on the base, an in-body install, declining the rule by name) are unchanged and still the owner's choice.

MEASURED at trunk dc4306f29.

    intersect.declaring({'minimum': 1},            Position.RULE)  ->  {}
    intersect.declaring({'maximum': 1},            Position.RULE)  ->  {}
    intersect.declaring({'minLength': 1},          Position.RULE)  ->  {}
    intersect.declaring({'pattern': 'x'},          Position.RULE)  ->  {}
    intersect.declaring({'contains': {...}},       Position.RULE)  ->  {'contains': {...}}
    intersect.declaring({'not': {'const': 'x'}},   Position.RULE)  ->  {'not': {...}}

So a BOUND and a PATTERN state nothing at RULE while every other constraint states itself. (`declaring` returns a mapping, not a list -- the earlier `-> []` reading was the old signature.) A bound constrains an instance; a reader asking "what does this node say about an instance" at RULE must hear it.

WHERE IT RIDES. `lower.rules._clauses` reads a condition through `_keys` (codegen/lower/rules.py:978), which is `declaring(..., RULE)`, and gates at rules.py:1181 on `keys <= {required, properties, type}`. A condition carrying a bound BESIDE required/properties would therefore pass the gate with the bound DROPPED -- and a `when` short of a conjunct holds MORE often than the document's condition, so the rule would require the consequent of instances the document does not. That is a rule refusing what the document admits, which section 2 forbids outright. `_values` has the same shape one level down: `{'const': 'x', 'minimum': 1}` reads keys `{'const'}` and returns `('x',)`.

IT IS NOT REACHED TODAY, re-measured, and that is why this is P2. Over the **1,059** portable documents of `patch.for_build(schemas/cache/3.2)` there are 591 `if` conditions, and **0** of them pass the gate while a constraining keyword is dropped. So the hole is latent and the first document to state a bound beside a condition's `required` would open it.

THE TWO SITES THE BOUND IS THE WHOLE PROPERTY DECLARATION AT, which is why this was found, both still present and both still declaring (the reduction returns `None` for the whole condition, which is the right answer either way -- a value RANGE is not a finite set the complement can be taken of):

    core/reporting-obligation.json#/allOf/0/if/properties/consumer_status_count         {'minimum': ...}
    core/reporting-reliability-statistics.json#/allOf/0/if/properties/official_revisions_adjusted  {'minimum': ...}
